---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../../components/SiteNav.astro';
import evidence from '../../data/logline-evidence.json';
/*
 * THE LOAD-BEARING SPLIT, copied from projects/telltale.astro. Figures are
 * typed as literals inside a data-ll span, so scripts/check-logline-evidence.cjs
 * does real work: a mistyped count fails the build. Frames are interpolated
 * from src/data/logline-evidence.json, so page-versus-record drift is
 * impossible by construction, which is the stronger guarantee for a
 * fixed-width render where one retyped space is invisible to a reader and
 * fatal to the alignment. Do not swap either for the other.
 */
const src = evidence.source;
const FRAME_ORDER = ['measure', 'nothing'];
const missing = FRAME_ORDER.filter((k) => !evidence.frames[k]);
if (missing.length) {
  throw new Error(`logline-evidence.json lacks frame(s): ${missing.join(', ')}. Re-run scripts/pull-logline-evidence.cjs.`);
}
---
<Base
  title="logline · A Ledger That Measures Its Own Claims · San Lee"
  description="A program ledger that measures its own claims against git. A milestone is met with a named commit, unmet, or unmeasured with a reason. It never renders a percent."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/projects/logline.html"
  ogType="article"
  ogImage="/assets/og-logline.png"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* A rendered report is an artifact, not prose: column alignment IS the
       content, so the shared `.article-body pre` rule (white-space: pre-wrap;
       overflow-wrap: anywhere) is turned off here. The selector out-ranks
       `.article-body pre` on specificity; a bare `.ll-frame` would lose and the
       failure is silent, because a wrapped frame cannot overflow and the mobile
       gate goes green. Same lesson, same fix, as `.tt-frame` on the telltale
       page. The frame is 80 columns and scrolls inside its own box. */
    .article-body pre.ll-frame {
      white-space: pre;
      overflow-x: auto;
      overflow-wrap: normal;
      max-width: 100%;
    }
    /* `main.wrap` is the breakout grid (ADR-008), so the figure is a grid item
       and a grid item's default `min-width: auto` refuses to shrink below its
       content. Both the item and the box need an explicit zero. */
    .ll-figure,
    .article-body pre.ll-frame { min-width: 0; }

    /* Plates 01 to 03. Page-scoped. Every text node is at or above 9px in a
       260-unit viewBox under the figure ladder's var(--fig-plate) cap, which
       renders at scale 1.046 at 320px, so the microtext floor holds without a
       measurement. No SVG text node carries a digit (figure contract, R-D).
       State is word plus geometry first, colour second. Oxide marks the
       negative finding: an UNMET claim on Plate 01, and each refused figure
       on Plate 02. Muted marks the state where nothing was measured. */
    .ll-plate .ll-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .ll-plate .ll-box-met { fill: var(--bg); stroke-width: 1.5; }
    .ll-plate .ll-box-unmet { stroke: var(--accent-warm); stroke-width: 1.5; }
    .ll-plate .ll-box-unmeasured { stroke: var(--muted); stroke-dasharray: 3 3; }
    .ll-plate .ll-tick {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-linecap: round;
      stroke-linejoin: round;
    }
    .ll-plate .ll-strike {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .ll-plate .ll-arrow {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .ll-plate .ll-state {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    }
    .ll-plate .ll-state-unmet { fill: var(--accent-warm); }
    .ll-plate .ll-state-unmeasured { fill: var(--muted); }
    .ll-plate .ll-value {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 600;
    }
    .ll-plate .ll-value-muted { fill: var(--muted); }
    .ll-plate .ll-value-bad { fill: var(--accent-warm); }
    .ll-plate .ll-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 9px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    .ll-plate-caption {
      max-width: var(--fig-plate);
      margin: 0.5rem auto 0;
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
    }
    /* The closing links are the only text of their block, so hit-target.cjs
       measures them against the 44px floor at 320 to 430px. */
    .ll-close .card-link { display: inline-block; }
    /* The ledger excerpt is prose-width JSON and may wrap; it is not a frame. */
    .ll-ledger { font-size: 0.8125rem; }
  </style>
  </Fragment>
  <SiteNav current="work" />

  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="../index.html">&larr; Back</a>
    <h1>logline</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A program ledger that measures its own claims. A milestone is met with a named commit, unmet, or unmeasured with a reason. It never renders a percent.</p>

    <div class="decision" aria-label="Case study summary">
      <p class="tag">Case study summary</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Result and evidence:</strong> The first cut reads a ledger of <span data-ll="claims.total">18</span> claims this site makes about <span data-ll="repos.count">6</span> public repositories and reports <span data-ll="claims.met">17</span> met, <span data-ll="claims.unmet">1</span> unmet, and <span data-ll="claims.unmeasured">0</span> unmeasured, each met claim beside the commit, tag or path that proves it. Named tests refuse a percent, a forecast and a verdict word, so a reader can grep for the refusals rather than trust them.</li>
        <li><strong>Problem:</strong> A status report renders a percent, a colour or a phrase, and none of those is a measurement. A percent needs a denominator nobody agreed on. A colour is a verdict with the evidence removed. I gate delivery on evidence, and no artifact on this site showed what that looks like as a tool.</li>
        <li><strong>Ownership:</strong> Sole owner. I set the three states, the list of refusals, and the rule that the first ledger is my own claims. Claude did most of the typing. The tests and the record are the proof.</li>
        <li><strong>Constraints:</strong> Git only, at a pinned ref, and never the working tree, so an uncommitted file cannot satisfy a claim. No network. One ref for every repository per run. A run that measured nothing exits with a failure code, because a report that measured nothing is not a report.</li>
        <li><strong>Actions:</strong> One Go binary with no dependencies. Five evidence kinds: a merged pull request, a tag, a path, a pattern count, a commit. A ledger of the claims this site already publishes. A puller and a gate on this site, so the counts on this page are re-read from the instrument and a drift fails the build.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- The measured report sits here, before the lead, so a phone reader sees
         the instrument before the argument. The text is interpolated from
         src/data/logline-evidence.json and carries no typed digit. -->
    <figure class="ll-figure fig" data-fig="frame" id="frame-measure" tabindex="-1">
      <span class="fig-label">Frame 01</span>
      <pre class="ll-frame" data-ll-frame="measure" tabindex="0">{evidence.frames.measure.lines.join('\n')}</pre>
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-what">
          The first ledger, measured. The header names the program, the date and
          the ref. The repos line names each repository at the commit it was
          read at. The count line is a count of states, which is a measurement.
          Each claim line opens with its state, and a met claim carries the
          commit or tag that proves it. Output of <code>logline measure</code>
          on <code>examples/public-system.json</code> at
          <code>{src.sha}</code>, copied byte for byte.
        </span>
        <span class="fig-limit">
          The frame shows one run on one machine on {src.pulledAt}.
          It does not show whether the claims were the right ones to make, and
          a met claim says a commit exists, not that the work behind it was
          good. The frame is 80 columns and scrolls sideways on a narrow screen.
        </span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p class="lead">
      logline is a Go program. It reads a file that lists the claims a program
      makes about itself, measures each one against git, and renders three
      words: met, unmet, unmeasured. The interesting part is what it will not
      render. A status report that fills a gap with a percent is worse than no
      report, because a reader trusts a percent. The engineering here is the
      code that refuses to compute one.
    </p>

    <!-- ADR-014: a bare plate takes a div wrapper, never a figure (gate law),
         and a hand-numbered label span. -->
    <div class="fig fig--plate">
    <span class="fig-label">Plate 01</span>
    <svg class="ll-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 100" role="img"
         aria-label="Three states a claim can be in. Met: a solid box with a commit id inside and a tick. Unmet: an oxide box, the measurement ran and found nothing. Unmeasured: a dashed grey box that names the reason the measurement could not run. Zero and absent never share a box.">
      <title>Met carries a commit. Unmet found nothing. Unmeasured could not run, and says why.</title>

      <rect class="ll-box ll-box-met" x="6" y="18" width="72" height="30" rx="2" />
      <text class="ll-value" x="42" y="37" text-anchor="middle">sha</text>
      <path class="ll-tick" d="M 14 24 L 18 28 L 26 20" />
      <text class="ll-state" x="42" y="64" text-anchor="middle">MET</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="42" y="78" text-anchor="middle">the commit</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="42" y="90" text-anchor="middle">that proves it</text>

      <rect class="ll-box ll-box-unmet" x="94" y="18" width="72" height="30" rx="2" />
      <text class="ll-value ll-value-bad" x="130" y="37" text-anchor="middle">nothing</text>
      <text class="ll-state ll-state-unmet" x="130" y="64" text-anchor="middle">UNMET</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="130" y="78" text-anchor="middle">the search ran</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="130" y="90" text-anchor="middle">and found nothing</text>

      <rect class="ll-box ll-box-unmeasured" x="182" y="18" width="72" height="30" rx="2" />
      <text class="ll-value ll-value-muted" x="218" y="37" text-anchor="middle">reason</text>
      <text class="ll-state ll-state-unmeasured" x="218" y="64" text-anchor="middle">UNMEASURED</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="218" y="78" text-anchor="middle">the search</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="218" y="90" text-anchor="middle">could not run</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="ll-plate-caption">
      A measurement that found nothing and a measurement that could not run are
      different claims, and the render keeps them apart. The second is the state
      a status report never admits to.
    </p>
    </div>

    <nav class="toc" id="toc" aria-labelledby="toc-heading">
      <p class="toc-heading" id="toc-heading">On this page</p>
      <ol>
      <li><a href="#what-it-is">What it is</a></li>
      <li><a href="#refusals">What it refuses to render</a></li>
      <li><a href="#ledger">The ledger is a file</a></li>
      <li><a href="#measurement">How a claim is measured</a></li>
      <li><a href="#first-ledger">The first ledger is this site&rsquo;s own claims</a></li>
      <li><a href="#limits">What it does not show</a></li>
      <li><a href="#built">How it was built</a></li>
      </ol>
