    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="what-it-is">What it is</h2>
    <p>
      A logline is the rope a ship paid out behind it to measure its own speed.
      Knots on the rope passed through a sailor&rsquo;s hand while a sandglass
      ran, and the count was the speed. Nobody estimated it. The name pairs
      with telltale, the ribbon on a sail, on purpose: two instruments, one
      rule.
    </p>
    <p>
      A technical program manager keeps a list of things the program says it
      has done or will do. A milestone shipped. A dependency cleared. A release
      cut. Every status report ever written renders that list as a colour, a
      percent or a phrase. logline renders it as three states, and every met
      state carries the evidence that earned it. The rule is one sentence: a
      claim may be met with a named commit, or it may be unmet, or it may be
      unmeasured with the reason. It may not be seventy percent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Zero and absent are different states and they never share a word. A
      pattern count that finds zero matches is unmet, with the count. A pattern
      count whose file does not exist at the ref is unmeasured, with the
      reason. That is the telltale rule applied to delivery instead of gauges,
      and it is the whole design.
    </p>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="refusals">What it refuses to render</h2>
    <div class="fig fig--plate">
    <span class="fig-label">Plate 02</span>
    <svg class="ll-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 96" role="img"
         aria-label="Three figures a status report renders and logline refuses, each struck through in oxide. A percent complete, because it needs a denominator nobody measured. An ETA, because it extrapolates. A green, because it is a verdict with the evidence removed.">
      <title>A percent, a forecast and a colour, each struck through. Each one is a number nobody measured.</title>

      <rect class="ll-box" x="6" y="16" width="72" height="30" rx="2" />
      <text class="ll-value ll-value-bad" x="42" y="35" text-anchor="middle">percent</text>
      <path class="ll-strike" d="M 12 31 L 72 31" aria-hidden="true" />
      <text class="ll-micro" x="42" y="62" text-anchor="middle">no denominator</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="42" y="74" text-anchor="middle">was measured</text>

      <rect class="ll-box" x="94" y="16" width="72" height="30" rx="2" />
      <text class="ll-value ll-value-bad" x="130" y="35" text-anchor="middle">ETA</text>
      <path class="ll-strike" d="M 100 31 L 160 31" aria-hidden="true" />
      <text class="ll-micro" x="130" y="62" text-anchor="middle">a forecast is</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="130" y="74" text-anchor="middle">not a reading</text>

      <rect class="ll-box" x="182" y="16" width="72" height="30" rx="2" />
      <text class="ll-value ll-value-bad" x="218" y="35" text-anchor="middle">green</text>
      <path class="ll-strike" d="M 188 31 L 248 31" aria-hidden="true" />
      <text class="ll-micro" x="218" y="62" text-anchor="middle">a verdict, and</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="218" y="74" text-anchor="middle">no evidence</text>

      <text class="ll-micro" x="130" y="90" text-anchor="middle">each refusal is a named test</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="ll-plate-caption">
      Each refusal is a test in the repository, not a sentence in a README. A
      renderer that prints one of these fails the build before anyone reads it.
    </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      I considered each of these, and I declined it. The argument for each one
      is short, and the test that holds the line is named.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No percent complete.</strong> A percent needs a denominator, and nobody has ever measured what one hundred percent of a program is. <code>TestRenderNeverPercent</code> reads every render on every fixture and fails on a single percent sign.</li>
      <li><strong>No ETA, velocity or burndown.</strong> Each one extrapolates. The render shows a declared due date beside a measured met date when both exist, and the signed difference in days, because that is arithmetic on two measured values and not a forecast.</li>
      <li><strong>No colour as the only signal.</strong> Every state is a word. The default render is ASCII, and it is the golden.</li>
      <li><strong>No verdict phrase.</strong> On track, at risk, green, healthy. <code>TestRenderNoVerdictWords</code> pins the list.</li>
      <li><strong>No inference across dependencies.</strong> A claim that needs an unmet claim is not marked blocked by inference. Its own evidence is measured on its own. The render prints the dependency&rsquo;s measured state beside it, and it flags one anomaly as a fact: a claim met before a claim it needs.</li>
      <li><strong>No report from nothing.</strong> A run in which every claim is unmeasured exits with code three. A report that measured nothing is a failed measurement, and this site has a whole page about checks that pass on work that never ran.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="ledger">The ledger is a file</h2>
    <p>
      JSON, because the standard library reads it and a ledger is edited by
      hand rarely. It names the program, the repositories it may cite, and the
      claims. Each claim carries an id, a title, an optional owner and due date,
      the ids it needs, and one piece of evidence.
    </p>
<pre class="ll-ledger">&#123;
  "program": "the public system",
  "repos": &#123;
    "kb-agent": &#123; "path": "../../kb-agent", "url": "https://github.com/sanlee-ys/kb-agent" &#125;
  &#125;,
  "claims": [
    &#123;
      "id": "kb-fanout-cap",
      "title": "kb-agent caps tool executions per round",
      "needs": ["kb-gold-set"],
      "evidence": &#123; "kind": "merged", "repo": "kb-agent", "pr": 111 &#125;
    &#125;
  ]
&#125;</pre>
    <p>
      Five evidence kinds, and each one names the git plumbing it runs. A
      <em>merged</em> claim looks for a commit reachable from the ref whose
      subject carries the pull request number. A <em>tag</em> claim resolves
      the tag and requires it to be an ancestor of the ref. A <em>path</em>
      claim lists the tree. A <em>grep</em> claim counts matches at the ref and
      needs a minimum. A <em>commit</em> claim resolves a sha and requires the
      same ancestry. Every read goes through git against a resolved ref and
      never the working tree, so an uncommitted file cannot satisfy a claim.
      The site&rsquo;s own evidence pullers follow the same rule, for the same
      reason.
    </p>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="measurement">How a claim is measured</h2>
    <div class="fig fig--plate">
    <span class="fig-label">Plate 03</span>
    <svg class="ll-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 84" role="img"
         aria-label="Four boxes in a row joined by arrows. Ledger, a file. Git, at a pinned ref. Record, a JSON file with a state per claim. Render, words first. The record is what the site's gate reads.">
      <title>Ledger to git to record to render. The record is what this site checks.</title>
      <rect class="ll-box" x="4" y="14" width="54" height="28" rx="2" />
      <text class="ll-state" x="31" y="32" text-anchor="middle">LEDGER</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="31" y="58" text-anchor="middle">a file,</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="31" y="70" text-anchor="middle">edited rarely</text>

      <path class="ll-arrow" d="M 58 28 L 68 28 M 65 25 L 68 28 L 65 31" aria-hidden="true" />

      <rect class="ll-box" x="70" y="14" width="54" height="28" rx="2" />
      <text class="ll-state" x="97" y="32" text-anchor="middle">GIT</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="97" y="58" text-anchor="middle">at a</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="97" y="70" text-anchor="middle">pinned ref</text>

      <path class="ll-arrow" d="M 124 28 L 134 28 M 131 25 L 134 28 L 131 31" aria-hidden="true" />

      <rect class="ll-box ll-box-met" x="136" y="14" width="54" height="28" rx="2" />
      <text class="ll-state" x="163" y="32" text-anchor="middle">RECORD</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="163" y="58" text-anchor="middle">one state</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="163" y="70" text-anchor="middle">per claim</text>

      <path class="ll-arrow" d="M 190 28 L 200 28 M 197 25 L 200 28 L 197 31" aria-hidden="true" />

      <rect class="ll-box" x="202" y="14" width="54" height="28" rx="2" />
      <text class="ll-state" x="229" y="32" text-anchor="middle">RENDER</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="229" y="58" text-anchor="middle">words,</text>
      <text class="ll-micro" x="229" y="70" text-anchor="middle">never colour</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="ll-plate-caption">
      The record is the product. A render replays a stored record byte for
      byte, and this site&rsquo;s gate reads the record rather than the screen.
    </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      <code>logline check</code> validates a ledger and prints every problem,
      not the first: a duplicate id, a dependency cycle, an unknown evidence
      kind, a date that does not parse. <code>logline measure</code> runs the
      check, resolves the ref in every repository, measures each claim, writes
      the record, and renders it. <code>logline render</code> renders a stored
      record with the same renderer, so a run is reproducible from its file.
      The record names the resolved commit of every repository, so a reader
      can re-run it and get the same lines.
    </p>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="first-ledger">The first ledger is this site&rsquo;s own claims</h2>
    <p>
      An instrument whose first ledger is a fixture proves the fixture. So the
      first ledger lists claims this site already publishes on its roadmap and
      its program page: pull requests by number in kb-agent, notes-api,
      architecture and this repository; the classifier artifact&rsquo;s tag; a
      telltale tag and its zero-versus-absent golden file; a Dockerfile where
      the site says one shipped. It also lists one Later row the site has not
      built, so the first report carries an unmet line and not only met lines.
      A ledger that only ever reports met is a badge.
    </p>
    <p>
      Frame 01 at the top of this page is that run. The counts in the summary
      are read from it by a script, and a gate on this site fails the build if
      a count on this page drifts from the record. The second frame is the
      same ledger pointed at an empty directory.
    </p>
    <figure class="ll-figure fig" data-fig="frame" id="frame-nothing" tabindex="-1">
      <span class="fig-label">Frame 02</span>
      <pre class="ll-frame" data-ll-frame="nothing" tabindex="0">{evidence.frames.nothing.lines.join('\n')}</pre>
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-what">
          The same ledger with no repository on disk. Every claim is unmeasured
          and every line says why. The count line still adds up. logline exits
          with code three on this run, because a report that measured nothing
          is a failed measurement. Output at <code>{src.sha}</code>, copied byte
          for byte.
        </span>
        <span class="fig-limit">
          This is a deliberate run against an empty directory, not a failure
          that happened in use. It shows the state; it does not show how often
          a real program lands in it.
        </span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="limits">What it does not show</h2>
    <p>
      logline measures git. It does not read issues, milestones, check runs or
      a chat thread, and a ledger cannot express a claim about a meeting or a
      decision that left no commit. One ref for every repository per run. The
      first ledger was written by one operator about his own repositories, and
      it names only public ones. A met claim says a commit exists. It does not
      say the work behind the commit was good, and it does not say the claim
      was the right one to make. The instrument shows whether a program&rsquo;s
      claims are backed by evidence. Which claims to make is still the job.
    </p>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="built">How it was built</h2>
    <p class="tradeoff">
      <strong>How it was built:</strong> I set the three states, the refusals,
      and the rule that the first ledger is my own claims. Claude did most of
      the typing. The proof is the named tests and the record this page is
      checked against.
      <a href="../colophon.html">How I built this site &rarr;</a>
    </p>
    <p class="ex-caption">
      A script built logline from <code>sanlee-ys/logline</code> at
      <code>{src.sha}</code> on <code>{src.pulledAt}</code>, ran it on
      <code>examples/public-system.json</code>, and wrote the counts and both
      frames on this page from its output. The ledger holds
      <span data-ll="claims.total">18</span> claims about
      <span data-ll="repos.count">6</span> repositories. A check in this
      site&rsquo;s test suite fails the build when a rendered figure drifts from
      that record.
    </p>

